WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.550
Most film productions shoot far more footage
than we ever see, and the stuff we do get

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.640
to see on the big screen is usually there
for a pretty good reason.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.860
But when the final edits are made, some deleted
scenes are sorely missed from the movies they

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:12.480
were cut from.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.170
They might add extra layers to the characters
and their stories, provide humorous breaks

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:21.380
in dramatic action, or even give the audience
a fascinating new perspective of the plot

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:21.880
itself.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.560
But these are not those scenes.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.440
No, these cinematic mistakes were better off
left on the cutting room floor.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.300
A dark turn

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.879
The Back to the Future movies were never supposed
to be anything but crackerjack, wholesome

00:00:33.879 --> 00:00:38.239
family fun, so it’s easy to see why this
particular scene in Part III ended up in the

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:38.780
trash.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.019
Early on in the movie, Buford Tannen's gang
face off with the local marshal and his young

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.600
son; after disarming the marshal, Tannen orders
him to leave town.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.280
He does, but Tannen shoots him in the back
anyway, right in front of the boy.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:51.520
“Pa!”

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.980
“Remember that word, son, discipline.”

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:03.280
While this might have been in keeping with
Tannen's ruthless, detestable character, it

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.320
would have been a shocking departure from
the trilogy’s lighthearted tone.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.260
Ant-Man wins big

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.780
Scott Lang’s desire to turn away from crime
and become a suitable role model for his young

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:17.119
daughter Cassie was one of Ant-Man’s principal
character arcs, so it’s no surprise, then,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:18.600
that this scene didn’t make the cut.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:23.540
It featured Scott’s buddies Kurt, Luis,
and Dave indulging in a night out at a casino,

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.680
consistently getting lucky with the help of
a certain diminutive hero.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.080
It’s an entertaining sequence, but given
Scott's steadfast insistence on turning over

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.180
a new leaf, it wouldn't have made a whole
lot of sense in the context of the movie.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.080
Hadley's lost hope

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:51.110
In James Cameron’s 1986 masterpiece Aliens,
Sigourney Weaver’s Ellen Ripley returns

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:54.840
to the moon LV-426 to make contact with a
lost human colony.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.299
Accompanied by a ragtag band of space marines,
Ripley finds the colony deserted and half-destroyed,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.800
resulting in some of the Alien saga’s darkest
and most unsettling scenes.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.920
“Nothing… not a g------ thing.”

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:14.319
The movie’s original cut, however, included
another scene which single-handedly put an

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.160
end to all the tension and terror of the abandoned
Hadley’s Hope.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.620
Here, we meet the inhabitants of the colony
as they go about their day, complete with

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.349
lengthy, unnecessary static shots and a walk-and-talk
conversation between two characters which

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:28.500
adds almost zero substance to the plot.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.800
We also meet Newt and her family, before the
scene concludes with the movie’s first appearance

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:40.600
of a facehugger.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:45.000
All in all, it amounts to an arduous, useless
bout of exposition which would have drained

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.240
pretty much all of the suspense from the movie’s
terrifying second act.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.000
Judgment revoked

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.780
The ending to Terminator 2: Judgement Day
is the perfect coda to a grim, thrilling sci-fi

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.121
adventure: the audience’s point of view
traverses a dark, mysterious road while a

00:03:00.121 --> 00:03:05.480
weary Sarah Connor muses on the ambiguity
of the future, before allowing herself a brief,

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.320
final glimmer of hope for humanity’s fate.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.100
James Cameron's original ending, however,
was a little less ambiguous.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.590
We see an elderly Sarah sitting in a park
while an adult John Connor plays with his

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:16.080
daughter.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.900
Sarah states flatly into a tape recorder that
Judgement Day has been and gone.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.580
“August 29th, 1997, came and went.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.620
Nothing much happened.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.820
Michael Jackson turned 40.”

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.040
She then makes a few comments on humanity’s
future which you might recognize from the

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:33.540
final cut.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.240
Fade to black; roll credits.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:39.320
It’s far too tidy an ending and totally
unsuitable for the movie it’s supposed to

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:39.820
wrap up.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.180
Then again, it wouldn’t have left much room
for a sequel either, which may not have been

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:45.260
the worst thing in the world.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.200
A Grievous error

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.579
The showdown between Obi-Wan Kenobi and his
wheezing alien nemesis General Grievous was

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.240
one of Revenge of the Sith’s more entertaining
sequences.

00:03:55.829 --> 00:03:59.800
According to a discarded animatic for the
scene, however, the fight almost ended in

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.600
the most ridiculous way possible.

00:04:02.129 --> 00:04:05.800
The scene would have played out largely the
same, right up to the moment that Kenobi manages

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.640
to partially pry open Grievous' protective
chestplate.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.880
Rather than getting cold-cocked and nearly
falling to his death, Kenobi would have finished

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.110
Grievous off by tearing out his heart with
his bare hands, casually tossing it aside

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:18.740
and blasting it with his sidearm.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:24.100
Somehow managing to be both way too dark and
cartoonish at the same time, the very fact

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:28.320
that this scene was cut from the movie is
quantifiable proof that George Lucas is sometimes

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.240
capable of discarding bad ideas.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.320
Not quite getting out

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:37.460
At the climax of Jordan Peele's masterpiece
Get Out, protagonist Chris Washington finally

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.720
escapes the grip of the nefarious Armitage
family.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:45.160
As he barely restrains himself from strangling
Rose, his ex-girlfriend and pursuer, a police

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:45.860
car arrives.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:51.220
Knowing nobody could possibly believe his
story, Chris prepares to be arrested, or worse,

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:51.860
but it's a fakeout.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.520
The car is driven by Chris' TSA buddy Rod,
who helps Chris make good his escape.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:00.531
In the movie’s original ending, however,
it really is the police who arrive, and Chris

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.360
is promptly arrested and taken to jail.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:03.500
“I’m good.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.120
I stopped it, you know?

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:11.340
I stopped it.”

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.600
Although it might have made for a more realistic
conclusion to the plot, Peele's decision to

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.720
give the beleaguered Chris a happy ending
seems like it was for the best.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:20.620
Clerks’ last shot

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.540
Kevin Smith's 1994 debut feature is a largely
plot-free film which found most of its comic

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:29.540
mileage in the reference-laden dialogue of
its slacker leads.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.160
It's a slight, sardonic work in which almost
nothing happens, which would have made its

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.500
alternate ending not only shocking, but completely
inappropriate.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.760
In the original ending, Dante is closing up
the store after Randal leaves.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.150
A man approaches the counter and Dante informs
him that they're closed, only to be shot dead

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:46.680
on the spot.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:51.440
As the man cleans out the register, the movie
ends with a close-up on Dante's lifeless body.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.920
Smith claimed that he shot this ending simply
because he had no idea how to end the movie,

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.240
but fortunately, star Brian O'Halloran, who
hated the sequence, was able to convince him

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:00.740
to cut it.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:04.260
Thank goodness, or the film might not have
become the cult classic it is today.

